 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a license\nto sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises",
    "basePrintNo": "S8225",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8225-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §64, ABC L",
    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8225\n\nSPONSOR: MONTGOMERY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in relation to a\nlicense to sell liquor at retail for consumption on certain premises\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill will allow for a restaurant, 33 Lafayette, LLC, to obtain a\nfull liquor license. The bill will amend the ABC 200-foot Law which will\nauthorize the issuing of a retail liquor license to a business in the\nCounty of Kings, Fort Greene that is within a two-hundred-foot proximity\nof a religious teaching institution.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Subdivision 7 of section 64 of the alcoholic beverage control\nlaw is amended by adding a new paragraph (e-12) to read as follows:\n(e-12)Notwithstanding the provisions of paragraph(a)of this subdivision,\nthe authority may issue a retail license for on-premises consumption for\na premises which shall be located within two hundred feet of a building\noccupied as a church, synagogue or other place of worship, provided such\npremises constitute a premises for the sale of food or beverages at\nretail for consumption on the premises located wholly within the bounda-\nries of the Borough of Brooklyn, county of Kings, bounded and described\nas follows:\n\nALL THAT CERTAIN plot, piece or parcel of land, with the buildings and\nimprovements thereon erected, situate, lying and being in the Borough of\nBrooklyn, County of Kings, City and State of New York, bounded and\ndescribed as follows: BEGINNING at a point on the northerly side of\nLafayette Avenue, distant 43 feet westerly from the corner formed by the\nintersection of The northerly side of Lafayette Avenue with the westerly\nside of St. Felix Street, which point is opposite the center line of a\nparty wall on the premises herein described and the premises adjoining\non the east;\n\nRUNNING THENCE westerly along the northerly side of Lafayette Avenue, 21\nfeet 6 inches to a point opposite the center line of another party wall\non the premises herein described and premises adjoining on the west;\n\nTHENCE northerly parallel with the westerly side of St. Felix Street,\nand part of the distance through a party wail, 100 feet to a point\ndistant 63 feet westerly (deed) 64 feet 6 inches (tax map) from the\nwesterly side of St. Felix Street measuring along a line drawn at right\nangles thereto from a point thereon distant 100 feet northerly from the\ncorner formed by the intersection of the northerly side of Lafayette\nAvenue with the westerly side of St. Felix Street;\n\nTHENCE easterly parallel with the northerly side of Lafayette Avenue and\nat right angles to St. Felix Street, 21 feet 6 inches; THENCE southerly\nparallel with St. Felix Street and part of the distance through a party\nwall, 100 feet to the northerly side of Lafayette Avenue at the point or\nplace of BEGINNING\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nMany urban communities across the State are facing an uphill battle when\nit comes to maintaining, developing and restoring their historic down-\ntown areas. Many of these historic areas and districts have existing\ninfrastructures where schools, theatres, universities and churches are\nin close proximity to various store fronts and restaurants. Fort Greene,\nthe neighborhood where 33 Wine Bar and Lounge Is located, is a destina-\ntion for culture and entertainment.  This authentic Haitian establish-\nment has served as the cultural mecca for Haitian cuisine in downtown\nBrooklyn. Because of the existing ABC 200-foot law, this establishment\nhas been restricted in fully serving its customers which makes it a\nchallenge to compete, thus impacting the future of business. BAM (Brook-\nlyn Academy of Music), which is directly across the street from 33\nLafayette, was recently issued liquor license in the theatres. Further-\nmore, the religious institution which is within 200-feet, Hanson Seventh\nDay Adventists Church and School, has expressed that they have no\nobjections to the request to apply for a full liquor license. The hours\nof operation for this establishment will not interfere or overlap during\nschool hours. An exemption to the ABC 200-foot Law would allow 33 Lafay-\nette to operate with a full liquor license they will be able to meet the\nneeds of the growing Haitian community and attract more patrons to the\nnew cultural center of Brooklyn by offering traditional Haitian cuisines\nthat include various types of liquor as well as cultural alcohol bever-\nages such as the famous Kremas, a dessert drink made with Haitian Rum.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
